[Music]  every friday during women's history  month we're taking a look at how the  coronavirus is affecting women and work  women in this country lost more than 5  million jobs over the past year  wow nearly 80 percent of those job  losses have been in government  retail education and health services and  leisure and hospitality services  they all have mostly female workforces  deborah flanagan worked as a travel  consultant helping companies coordinate  travel programs for their employees  and this is her story  i've been in the travel industry for 12  years i travel a lot so  it really was a good combination of the  things that i enjoy doing  when the pandemic hit debra flanagan  knew her  job was on the line by april the travel  industry was decimated  and she was laid off a call came in and  based on what i had seen going on in the  industry and i knew  kind of what the call was it was just a  little bit of shock but  it was it was mostly expected flanagan's  unemployment checks  and her husband's income kept the family  afloat  did you get all your homework done but  she had to make some changes  we just started cutting costs looked at  utilities and tried to  switch providers and do whatever we  could to  save a little bit i kind of immediately  got  you know my resume together there's  nothing under travel consulting it's  been really hard it comes up  empty quite a bit it's a really kind of  dead market right now deborah has now  turned her attention to building a  community newsletter  because she doesn't know if or when she  can ever return to the travel industry  going back into the industry it's going  to be a heck of a lot more competition  it's going to be a much harder climb  back  the unknown it's hard  it is so hard linkedin has been studying  this issue for its series it's called  conversations for change  carolyn fairchild is editor at large of  linkedin's global news team and joins us  this morning  carolyn it's really good to see you  there are a lot of deborah flanagan's  out there a lot of people are telling  the same story  so what do you think employers need to  do to keep women in the workforce  well good morning gail i'm glad that you  asked that question the pandemic has had  an outsized impact on the careers of  tens of millions of professionals what  we're finding is that women  particularly women of color are burying  the brunt of this crisis  there are 2.3 million deborah flanagan's  out there who are now  not in the workforce and what that means  is that women's participation rate in  the workforce  right now is at its lowest rate since  1988. so if employers don't act now  don't think about flexible workplace  policies don't check in with the current  women that they have in their workforce  we're risking decades of progress being  erased from women in terms of what  they've done in the workforce  since 1988. so carolyn i want to talk  about how we get women  back into the workforce so for people  who have lost their job during this  pandemic and how do they talk about that  job loss and that gap in their resume  when they go back out there on the  market  sure particularly for women career gaps  have been something that have been  stigmatized when it comes to returning  to work but the good thing about this  pandemic  is that that stigma has virtually gone  away we hear from hiring managers on  linkedin who say  they almost expect career gaps because  of the devastating impact this pandemic  has had on the labor force so  walk in confident knowing that that's  the case don't feel bad about it  actually come in with a case for  what you've done during this time the  skills that you've gained maybe you've  taken courses  that will help you with your next job  but really own the fact that this has  been a hard time for everyone and know  that you're not alone so carolyn there's  also  a difficult moment people face when  they're looking for a job particularly  looking for a long time and failing  at what point do people say i need to  change careers not just keep trying to  apply in the same area  we have data from linkedin right now  that's showing that women in particular  right now given just the devastating  impact that  the economy has had right now in  industries where we see a lot of women  you mentioned some of those industries  travel retail food and beverage these  are industries that have more women than  men in the workforce right now  and they're having to pivot and they're  thinking about different career paths  that they can go on and i think that  right now  the name of the game is skills how can  you transfer your resume from just a job  and an industry to the skills that you  have  so you can make that pivot into the  industries that are booming  you also say that it's all about  storytelling it's good to hear that  employers aren't judging you if there's  a career gap because when i was coming  it  up it was it was always said you don't  want to have a career gap  it doesn't look good a time out so you  say that it's about storytelling what  does that mean in getting a job  you have to sell yourself  it means that we have to rethink how  people think about their careers so many  people think okay it's my job title it's  the industry that i'm in  what we're seeing on linkedin in the new  digital economy is that it's about your  skills  transferable skills that you have let's  just take debra for example  the travel industry isn't a great place  to be working right now but her  experience  as a consultant that can be transferred  into e-commerce it can be transferred  into business sales  these are skills that she has that she  can then say and her resume painted out  as a story as you just said gail  in terms of getting her next opportunity  all right carolyn fairchild leading a  very  important conversation for us carolyn  thank you very much we hope it works out  for people who are listening

